While building the three-axis rating system, I verified that PUT /jokes/<id>/ratings/me is idempotent—sending the same JSON multiple times results in the same database state—while POST /jokes/7/ratings attempts to create new rows. Initially, duplicate POST requests both returned 201 Created, exposing the missing UniqueConstraint('user_id','joke_id') in the model. After adding the constraint, the second request triggered an IntegrityError and correctly returned 409 Conflict. This demonstrated how HTTP verb choice and database constraints encode idempotency and defense in depth directly into the system.
